  "author_guidance": "In the 24-round test these moves were never possible for anyone: make-leads (The lead maker has graphite: was 0, needed at least 1; business.graphite is raised only by mine-graphite (on its actor), each on the one who does it, so no action ever brings business.graphite to this actor; The lead maker has clay: was 0, needed at least 1; nothing ever raises business.clay); make-pencils (The factory has a lead: was 0, needed at least 1; business.leads is raised only by make-leads (on its actor), each on the one who does it, so no action ever brings business.leads to this actor; The factory has a slat: was 0, needed at least 1; business.slats is raised only by saw-slats (on its actor), each on the one who does it, so no action ever brings business.slats to this actor); pack-box (The packer has a pencil: was 0, needed at least 1; business.pencils is raised only by make-pencils (on its actor), each on the one who does it, so no action ever brings business.pencils to this actor; The packer has a box: was 0, needed at least 1; business.boxes is raised only by supply-materials (on its actor), each on the one who does it, so no action ever brings business.boxes to this actor); saw-slats (The sawmill has a log: was 0, needed at least 1; business.logs is raised only by cut-cedar (on its actor), each on the one who does it, so no action ever brings business.logs to this actor); sell-pencil (The shop has a pencil: was 0, needed at least 1; business.pencils is raised only by make-pencils (on its actor), each on the one who does it, so no action ever brings business.pencils to this actor). For each, make sure some other action produces what its checks need (for example, if goods must be at the next stop before they can move on, give someone an action that brings them there). Nothing in the world ever makes or brings in these, and everyone starts with none, so every step that uses them is impossible: clay. Give each one a source: an actor whose action makes or supplies it, or a process that renews it at the place it comes from. These goods are made or supplied but no move ever takes them from where they pile up, so the step that needs them never gets them: boxes piles up at supply-maker (supply-materials); erasers piles up at supply-maker (supply-materials); ferrules piles up at supply-maker (supply-materials); graphite piles up at graphite-miner (mine-graphite); leads piles up at lead-maker (make-leads); logs piles up at loggers (cut-cedar); pencils piles up at pencil-factory (make-pencils); slats piles up at sawmill (saw-slats). Give someone a move that carries each one to the place that uses it. These moves use up a good where it never arrives, so they can never happen: make-leads needs clay at lead-maker, which starts with none and nothing ever gives it any; make-leads needs graphite at lead-maker, which starts with none and nothing ever gives it any; make-pencils needs erasers at pencil-factory, which starts with none and nothing ever gives it any; make-pencils needs ferrules at pencil-factory, which starts with none and nothing ever gives it any; make-pencils needs leads at pencil-factory, which starts with none and nothing ever gives it any; make-pencils needs slats at pencil-factory, which starts with none and nothing ever gives it any; pack-box needs boxes at box-packer, which starts with none and nothing ever gives it any; pack-box needs pencils at box-packer, which starts with none and nothing ever gives it any; saw-slats needs logs at sawmill, which starts with none and nothing ever gives it any; sell-pencil needs pencils at shop, which starts with none and nothing ever gives it any. Give the holder a source: the step that makes or carries the good must add it there (a delivery that counts down must add the goods to the receiver when it ends).",
